00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.350 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I have with me Mike Makai of Openreach  who's just presented at the FTTH  conference here in Amsterdam Mike nice  to talk to you again one of the things  you were talking about was open reach a  lots happened in the last 12 months yes  in the last 12 months we finished the  separation of open reach from BT as per  the agreement that BT reached with Ofcom  during 2017 the last major milestone  that we achieved was the transfer over a  thirty-one thousand employees to the  newly formed open reach limited from  from BT itself was the biggest 2p  transfer of people that's ever been  undertaken in the UK to the best of our  knowledge so it's a really big deal so  we feel now that we are fully  independent to the degree that's been  agreed between Ofcom and BT we started  to have a very different approach to the  way in which we talk with our customers  with our stakeholders we feel that we're  more engaged with them and we have  developed I think and recovered a lot of  the trust and so forth but it's very  necessary with with you know with one's  customers so we're very satisfied with  the progress that we've made and we've  done all that at the same time as  improving the service that we offer to  to our end customers and to our  customers in the marketplace and one of  the things you you asked for it in your  talk was a bit of help from the UK  government tell us a bit more about that  yeah that's right so we're fully  committed to be the you know the UK's  access network provider as we move into  the new world of fibre to the premises  and we've we made that announcement  about this time last year and we are  definitely on with that building at  spite pace and scale and we very much  want to provide the lion's share of the  government's ambitions which you may  recall by 2025 they want to have 15  million homes and businesses passed and  by 2033 they want to have the whole of  the UK fully Fiber those are very very  aggressive targets we're putting  together a business case at the moment  to help us you know support those those  targets and one of the areas where we  are looking for help from government is  in the  rateable value or the tax that they  charge on the network assets that we  build so at the moment there is a tax a  rate called cumulates which is applied  to all network assets as they are  actually built not when they become  revenue generating but as they are built  and clearly if you when you've got a  business case that takes 20 years to pay  back all the costs that you're paying  out before it starts to pay back you  know our are an impediment to actually  making the case to invest so what we've  asked government is that they consider  an exemption to all new fiber build for  a 20-year period from 2021 and and that  would significantly help us in terms of  improving our business case that we need  to take to our board and then take to  our shareholder bTW absolutely well  let's hope they'll be cheered by the  news that for the first time the UK is  in the global rankings you've played a  major part in that give us a reaction  I'm very very pleased that that's  happened I mean I think it was a bit of  an embarrassment that we weren't there  in the first place but here we are and I  think we will go from strength to  strength  you know we ourselves as you may have  heard me speak earlier we've doubled you  know our installed base of fiber in the  last 12 months which you know we built  more in the last 12 months than we had  built in the history of the company up  until that time and we will continue to  do that we are you know we're building  at a rate of about 14,000 that's a small  town in the UK 14,000 homes and  businesses a week at the moment and we  intend to continue to ramp that up so  that we can provide the lion's share of  the government's ambition that's  fantastic news Mike thank you very much  for talking to us thanks for your time  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

